  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nCurrently...\n\nQuiet, but toasty conditions reign across the forecast area this \nafternoon...with satellite imagery showing plenty of sunshine in\nplace. Looking in the upper levels...a ridge of high pressure \nremains the main feature for much of the central/western CONUS, \nroughly centered over the central Rockies. The ridging extends \nup into SW portions of Canada...with satellite imagery showing a\nlow pressure system working its way east into the western Great\nLakes region this afternoon. This upper level systems is \nhelping drive a surface cold front south through the \nPlains...which is currently sitting across northern NE up into \nSErn SD and MN. Ahead of this front...temps have had little \nissue climbing well into the 90s to right around 100. Making \nthings miserable this afternoon are the dewpoints, which \ncurrently sit in the mid 60s-low 70s...resulting in widespread \nheat index values over 100 degrees. No changes made to the CWA-\nwide Heat Advisory...which remains in effect until 10PM this \nevening. \n\nLate this afternoon through Tuesday...\n\n\nThis sfc cold front will continue to sink south through the\nregion this evening and tonight...and while it's arriving too \nlate to provide any help with today's temperatures, it may aid \nin sparking off some thunderstorm activity later this afternoon \nand evening. Satellite imagery showing a line of CU developing \nfrom west-east along that boundary, barely along our far \nnorthern edge. The better upper forcing is lacking across our \narea...but a few isolated storms are not out of the question. \nWhile MUCAPE values across the area are not lacking...any storms\nwill have to work against weaker deeper layer shear and warm \nmid-level temps, so they may struggle to maintain strength. The \narea is currently not outlooked by SPC for severe weather...but \nany stronger storms will be capable of gusty winds, small hail \nand heavy rainfall rates. \n\nThrough the overnight hours and into the morning hours on \nTuesday...there is some disagreement among models with whether\nor not we see a second batch of activity develop. Model timing\nin decent agreement showing the sfc front clearing the CWA to \nthe south between 09-12Z...with this isolated-scattered activity\ndeveloping along an elevated front lagging behind across \nnorthern portions of the area. Because there are differences \nwith whether or not it occurs..kept PoPs on the lower side after\n03Z around 20 percent. Have chances ending mid-morning Tuesday \nwith the rest of the day dry...but again there are some \nuncertainties in models, some showing at least a few isolated \nshowers could linger longer into the day. Even if activity does \nlinger...not looking at appreciable rainfall amounts. Behind the\ncold front, the more notable changes will be to the winds, \nwhich look to be northeasterly through the day...and \ntemperatures/dew points. After the widespread 90s-low 100s \ntoday, forecast highs for Tuesday remain in the upper 80s in the\nNNE to mid 90s....not a huge change, but add in forecast \ndewpoints in the 50s- near 60 vs the 60s-70s today...it makes a \ndifference.\n\nMid-week on through the weekend...\n\nBetter chances for precipitation do finally return to the \nforecast...mainly during the Wednesday-Thursday night time\nframe. Not expecting a non-stop rain-out or the like during \nthat entire period...these chances coming more in a couple of \nwaves. Models show the first of these shortwave upper level \ndisturbance working into WNW portions of the area perhaps \nalready as early late Tuesday night...with the better chances \nthrough the day on Wednesday focused more across the NNWrn half \nof the forecast area, with the disturbance riding more along the\nedge of the ridging/NWrly flow. Disturbances moving through \nlater Wednesday night through Thursday look to have the \npotential to dig further south into the ridging while crossing \nthe Rockies, helping spread those better chances further south. \nWill have to see how things trend in the coming days, at this \npoint the overall severe threat is on the lower side...the area \nnot being included in any SPC outlooks through mid-week. \n\nAs we get into the end of the week-upcoming\nweekend...precipitation chances look to be more sparse...as \nmodels show that upper level ridging reamplifying across \ncentral/western portions of the CONUS. \n\nAs far as temperatures go...with the better precipitation\nchances around, forecast highs Wed-Thu are mainly in the \n70s-80s...climbing back well into the 90s by the end of the \nweekend.",
